WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.730
RL: There is fantastic, rich complexity in the world around us.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.660
But the beauty and the cruelty of existence

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.130
is not something we can always handle alone.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.350
That struggle to survive extends beyond humans

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.130
and includes all life on the planet, where

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.300
a seemingly endless menagerie of creatures

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.680
live, compete, and sometimes help one another as they populate the Earth.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.910
Even the very smallest are an important part of this web of life.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.950
But how did this come to be?

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:56.870
For us to find out, let&amp;#39;s go back to a time of ancient competition

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:03.240
and see how a simple prokaryotic cell-- no more than a membrane, nucleic acids,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.225
and proteins-- evolved a remarkably beneficial relationship to help

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:08.725
it thrive.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.370
Billions of years ago, these early cells lived in rich waters,

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:19.700
searching for energy.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.850
Some cells consumed energy from molecules directly.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:34.240
Some of those cells became energy sources for others,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.280
while still others cells evolved the ability

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.360
to use sunlight to split water molecules as an energy source.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.660
But the pure oxygen released in this reaction

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.760
changed the very atmosphere of the planet

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.800
and likely harmed other prokaryotes.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.900
Many didn&amp;#39;t survive.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.770
But what&amp;#39;s this?

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:05.630
Here is a cell that evolved to use oxygen as a powerful energy source,

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.750
allowing it to thrive in this new oxygen-rich environment.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.240
But the cell still competed for survival in a dangerous world.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.815
Then something remarkable happened.

00:02:17.815 --> 00:02:22.180
A larger cell engulfed our little oxygen-using cell.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.560
But this time, the little cell survived inside its host.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.560
Now the smaller cell could harness energy from oxygen,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.770
but this time from the safety of its new home--

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:37.940
even more energy than it needed, allowing it to share energy

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.410
with the larger host cell.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:47.150
In return, the host cell provided nutrients and safety from predators.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.210
This relationship was simply too good to end.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.340
Over generations, the two cells&amp;#39; descendants

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.340
exchanged DNA and became dependent on each other for survival,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.980
living as one organism in an evolutionary process

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.900
called endosymbiosis.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.810
This mutually-beneficial relationship only deepened over time,

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:15.060
allowing the evolution of what we know today as the eukaryote,

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.720
with a true nucleus and organelles that complete specialized tasks.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.340
And what about that little oxygen-consuming cell?

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.010
It evolved into the mitochondrion, which still harnesses

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.110
most of the energy in eukaryotic cells.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.780
In your own body right now, a mitochondrion

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.020
can maintain an electric field across its membrane

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.210
of 30 million volts per meter, a potential that

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:46.180
is equal to the energy released by a bolt of lightning in the sky.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.370
This energy supported the evolution of even more structures

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:56.490
in more diverse combinations, changing and building on one another over time.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.260
All of the varied multicellular life on earth

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.810
derives from that cooperative relationship in our ancient past.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:09.590
Had it not been for this chance relationship so long ago, life on Earth

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.120
wouldn&amp;#39;t exist as it does today.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.040
It all began with cells evolving to cooperate and create

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.190
new possibilities for life.

